searchtable=table('tab:search_top5_combined','Top five severity configurations by validation balanced accuracy, seed 1234.',['Stream','Rank','D','F','BN','Dr','LR','WD','BS','Val. BA (\\%)'],rows,width=True,note='D: depth; F: filters per branch; BN: bottleneck width; Dr: dropout; LR: learning rate; WD: weight decay; BS: batch size. Test scores are not used for ranking. All 12 selected task/stream/repeat configurations are supplied in the revision materials.')
sub('sec:training_details',r'''A fixed sample of 100 candidate configurations was evaluated independently for each task (binary detection or four-class severity), sensing stream, and recording split, giving 1,200 main search fits. Candidate ranges were depth 3/4/6, filters 16/24/32/48, bottleneck width 8/16/32, dropout 0.20/0.35/0.50, learning rate $5\times10^{-4}$ or $10^{-3}$, weight decay $5\times10^{-5}$ or $10^{-4}$, and batch size 64 or 128. The sampled candidate set, rather than every Cartesian combination, was searched. Training used Adam, inverse-frequency weighted cross-entropy, a 60-epoch cap, and early stopping after 10 epochs without validation-balanced-accuracy improvement. A plateau scheduler halved the learning rate after three unimproved epochs, down to $10^{-6}$. Checkpoints and hyperparameters were selected only by validation balanced accuracy; ties followed the fixed candidate order. Table~\ref{tab:search_top5_combined} illustrates the ranking for one specified split.

Reported test classification metrics are mean $\pm$ sample standard deviation over the three holdouts. Precision, recall, and F1 are macro-averaged; balanced accuracy is the mean class recall. Because the same recordings can recur across repeats, these standard deviations describe split/initialization sensitivity rather than uncertainty from independent physical replications. Test results were not used to select configurations or feature subsets. One-factor sensitivity experiments changed depth to 3/9, dropout to 0.1/0.4, or learning rate to $3\times10^{-4}/3\times10^{-3}$ around each selected configuration; all other settings were retained (Fig.~\ref{fig:hyperparameter_sensitivity}).

'''+searchtable)
sub('sec:domain_adaptation',r'''Source-only transfer was evaluated in both directions, ACIM $\rightarrow$ PMSM and PMSM $\rightarrow$ ACIM. For each direction, six source recordings were used for training and three for validation. The same 100-candidate search was performed using source validation only, independently of mixed-domain model selection. Six target recordings formed the adaptation pool and the remaining three formed the target test set. Thus, no source or target recording crosses its fitting/evaluation boundary.

Nominal adaptation budgets of 1\%, 5\%, 10\%, and 20\% select 2, 9, 18, or 36 complete episode groups from the 180-group target pool; the first realized group fraction is 1.11\%. For sampling only, each post-schedule normal tail is attached to its parent recording's final episode group while retaining its normal label and actual timestamp. Consequently, window fractions can differ from nominal group fractions. Fine-tuning updates the source model for 20 fixed epochs at 0.2 times its selected learning rate, with source normalization frozen. No target validation labels or target-test labels are used. Target-only controls are trained from random initialization on exactly the same labeled target subsets for 20 epochs, using the selected architecture, its original learning rate, and normalization fitted on that subset. These are controls under the stated training budget, not exhaustive target-only optimization. All conditions use three seeds.''')
