sub('sec:adaptation_results',r'''Table~\ref{tab:domain_adaptation_anomaly2} reports binary balanced accuracy for all target budgets, including matched target-only controls. At 20\%, motor fine-tuning reaches 75.99\% for ACIM-to-PMSM and 92.63\% for PMSM-to-ACIM transfer. PCC fine-tuning reaches 56.30\% and 91.38\%, respectively, preserving a pronounced direction asymmetry.

Fine-tuning is not uniformly preferable to target-only training. For motor ACIM-to-PMSM transfer, the 20\% target-only control reaches 89.05\%, exceeding fine-tuning at 75.99\%. For PCC PMSM-to-ACIM transfer, fine-tuning reaches 91.38\%, compared with 83.60\% for target-only training. Thus, the value of source pretraining depends on sensing location, direction, and target-label budget. These observations concern the stated fixed-epoch controls and do not establish a universal advantage of transfer learning.

'''+table('tab:domain_adaptation_anomaly2','Binary adaptation and matched target-only controls: balanced accuracy (mean $\\pm$ SD, \\%).',['Stream','Direction','Training','Source only','1\\%','5\\%','10\\%','20\\%'],rows,width=True,note='Budgets are nominal fractions of the target episode-group pool, not fractions of the entire target dataset. Source-only and adapted models share the source-selected configuration within each repeat.'))
sub('sec:error_analysis',r'''Fig.~\ref{fig:main_confusion_matrices} shows the full error distributions. Binary motor sensing combines high attack recall with a substantial and variable normal false-alarm rate; PCC sensing misses more attacks. For the PCC severity model, mistakes include both normal/attack confusion and confusion among nonzero bias bins. Therefore, the errors should not be characterized solely as confusion between adjacent levels. Exact-severity and any-attack recall are reported together in Appendix~\ref{app:severity_detection}.

Fig.~\ref{fig:case1_30_motor_pcc_detection_examples} shows the complete held-out ACIM recording, including the post-schedule normal tail. The recording was selected by a fixed alphabetical rule, not for visual agreement with the schedule. The trace exposes both successful detections and false alarms. A corresponding held-out PMSM trace is supplied with the revision materials.''')
