sub('sec:latency_throughput',r'''Runtime was measured on the Intel Core Ultra 9 285K CPU and NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPU using the seed-1234 binary models. Each variant used 10 warm-up executions and 50 timed repetitions. The benchmark separates feature extraction, normalization/transfer/inference, and full processing, reporting median and 95th-percentile latency (Table~\ref{tab:runtime_latency}). End-to-end processing excludes the 200-ms acquisition window; it is not an attack-onset-to-alarm delay. CPU and GPU-assisted versions use the same selected models.

Full-feature GPU-assisted processing takes 22.37~ms for PCC and 66.33~ms for motor sensing, with 95th percentiles of 23.93 and 67.55~ms. Both measured processing times are below the 200-ms window interval. Reciprocal median processing rates are approximately 44.69 and 15.08 windows/s, respectively; these are workstation processing capacities, not measured embedded worst-case guarantees. Acquisition, scheduling, and any alarm-persistence logic must be included in a deployed timing budget.

'''+table('tab:runtime_latency','Full-feature processing latency, excluding 200-ms acquisition.',['Stream','Execution','Stage','Median (ms)','95th pct. (ms)'],rows,width=True))
