sub('sec:lfhf_ablation',r'''All seven LF/HF/TD combinations were evaluated by retraining with the selected full-feature architecture fixed within each task, stream, and split. Table~\ref{tab:lfhf_ablation_anomaly2} reports binary results. For PCC, LF+Time reaches 80.03\% balanced accuracy, close to the full input at 80.29\%, while reducing GPU-assisted processing from 22.37 to 12.32~ms (1.82$\times$). Thus, the measured marginal PCC benefit of HF features does not establish that their extra computation is necessary for binary detection on this dataset.

For motor sensing, time-only reaches 92.89\% balanced accuracy and HF+Time reaches 94.11\%, compared with 88.26\% for the full input. Time-only processing takes 16.28~ms (4.07$\times$ faster than full). HF-only performs poorly for both streams (63.76\% motor and 64.49\% PCC balanced accuracy), but its combination with time features is useful for motor sensing. These are observed ablation results, not test-based selection of a new primary model. Severity ablations and complete per-repeat measurements are supplied with the revision materials.

'''+table('tab:lfhf_ablation_anomaly2','Binary LF/HF/TD ablation (mean $\\pm$ SD, \\%).',['Stream','Features','Number','Accuracy','Balanced acc.','Macro F1'],rows,width=True))
# Discussion keeps the original headings and order, with restrained interpretation.
sub('sec:adaptation_asymmetry',r'''The PCC results retain a strong direction asymmetry: at 20\% target labels, PMSM-to-ACIM fine-tuning reaches 91.38\% balanced accuracy, while ACIM-to-PMSM reaches 56.30\%. Source-only predictions also show different class biases: for PCC ACIM-to-PMSM, the mean attack miss rate is 92.71\%; in the reverse direction the normal false-alarm rate is 86.03\%. These biases show that source-domain decision boundaries and operating distributions do not transfer reliably. They do not isolate a causal mechanism for the asymmetry.

The target-only comparison is essential. PCC PMSM-to-ACIM fine-tuning exceeds its matched target-only control, whereas motor ACIM-to-PMSM fine-tuning is worse than its control at 20\%. Source pretraining can therefore help or hinder adaptation under the stated budget. The results motivate target-domain calibration and direction-specific evaluation; they do not show that any pretrained motor model is suitable for another installation.''')
sub('sec:pcc_vs_motor',r'''Motor sensing provides higher mean class-balanced performance on this dataset, particularly for distinguishing injected-bias bins. PCC sensing reduces terminal instrumentation but aggregates the responses of both drive branches and their shared converter interface. For the four-class PCC model, relatively high any-attack recall at medium/high bias coexists with low exact-severity recall and frequent false alarms. The separate binary detector is the stronger primary PCC alarm model in these experiments.

The motor-type task is an auxiliary diagnostic of distinguishable recording domains, not proof of a causal motor-physics explanation: the motor families also have different load-setting ranges and share only one installation per family. Attack-only regression likewise estimates injected bias rather than torque or damage (Appendix~\ref{app:auxiliary_diagnostics}). For efficiency, the PCC LF+Time representation offers a measured alternative to full features with similar balanced accuracy. Neither classification scores nor processing latency alone establish safe automatic mitigation.''')
sub('sec:limitations',r'''The evidence is limited to additive, piecewise-constant current-feedback bias on one two-branch laboratory platform. Monitoring sensors are assumed uncompromised; coordinated attacks on those sensors, replay, denial of service, command attacks, and stealthy time-varying perturbations were not tested. Three fixed speed settings and three load settings were recorded for each machine, but dedicated speed ramps, load steps, and other dynamic operating conditions were not evaluated. Brake excitation identifies operating settings; calibrated shaft torque and mechanical harm were not measured.

PCC signatures can depend on the shared rectifier, downstream inverter topology, control behavior, modulation, sensor transfer functions, and supply impedance. These factors were not varied independently in the dataset. Consequently, no robustness claim is made for other converter topologies, modulation strategies, grids, or installations. Whole-recording holdouts remove within-repeat recording/episode overlap, but remain a retrospective evaluation on reused data from the same apparatus. Three repeated holdouts do not provide independent hardware replication or a strong significance test.

The adaptation study requires target labels and uses fixed training budgets; unsupervised adaptation and exhaustive target-only optimization are outside its scope. PCC severity remains weak, and normal false alarms are material even when attack recall is high. Finally, measured workstation processing times exclude acquisition and do not establish embedded worst-case latency or a tested closed-loop mitigation system.

'''+fig('fig:app_attack_process_examples','figure8_raw_rms','Representative held-out ACIM and PMSM recordings. Top: documented maximum injected phase bias; middle/bottom: RMS of one motor-current and PCC-current channel in recorded acquisition units. The displayed interval covers the scheduled episodes and zero-bias tail. These traces illustrate operating responses, not calibrated torque, and their visual differences are not evidence of a causal transfer mechanism.'))
sub('sec:conclusion',r'''This paper assessed nonintrusive FDIA monitoring in a heterogeneous ACIM/PMSM inverter-fed drive platform using recording- and episode-disjoint evaluation. Across three holdouts, binary accuracy was 87.41\% for motor-side sensing and 80.78\% for PCC sensing. Four-class injected-bias balanced accuracy was 90.81\% and 55.62\%, respectively, demonstrating a substantial limitation of PCC severity estimation. Severity-conditioned attack recall complements exact-bin recall but must be interpreted alongside false alarms. Cross-motor source-only transfer was unreliable, and supervised adaptation benefits depended on direction and sensing location; matched target-only controls prevented attributing every gain to pretraining. PCC LF+Time retained approximately full-feature binary balanced accuracy while reducing processing from 22.37 to 12.32~ms on the tested workstation. The results support further development of PCC monitoring for additive feedback-bias attacks while delimiting its accuracy, calibration, and deployment constraints.''')
